EXT. SCHOOL ROOFTOP - SUNSET

Gravel, a rusted vent, a chain-link railing. The football field glows green below.

JAMAL OKONKWO, 16, in a band jacket, sits cross-legged by the edge, a trombone case across his knees. SUNNY PARK, 16, arrives through the stairwell door, drops her backpack, kicks a pebble off the roof, and steps up beside him as the two of them watch it drop, Jamal tapping the case, Sunny pulling her hair into a bun, the pair silent while the marching band warms up below.

SUNNY
(to the field)
Hey, losers!

No one hears. Jamal smiles.

SUNNY (CONT'D)
You're not going to practice?

JAMAL
(muttering)
Later.

SUNNY
That's what you said Tuesday.

JAMAL
Tuesday was a mess.

She sits. Her shoulder bumps his. He doesn't move away.

SUNNY
Mr. Dalrymple asked about you.

JAMAL
What did you say?

SUNNY
That you're sick.

JAMAL
Am I?

SUNNY
You look sick.

He grins. She takes a gum wrapper from her pocket and folds it into a tiny swan.
